  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe forecast area remains in the warm advection region of the\nupper low, but dry air has moved in thru the mid levels. The\nresult is plenty of mist/drizzle in addition to scattered\nshowers. This is expected to continue into the evening until the\ncenter of the upper low approaches. Deeper moisture returns and\nan area of showers is forecast to lift northeast thru the\nforecast area. As southwest flow picks up tonight some gradually\nscattering out and lifting of the low clouds will occur. More\nlikely will be that the true clearing out does not happen until\nwinds become more northwesterly Sat as the upper low center\ndrifts northeast of the area. Upslope rain and snow showers will\ncontinue Sat with temps returning to near/above normal. \n\nSat evening into the overnight a trailing vorticity maximum\nmoves across the Northeast. Along and north of the track a\nrelatively narrow band of precip is likely to form. Temps will\nbe marginal but some light rain or snow is possible. At this\ntime most of the forecast area is rain, but it would not take\nthe lower levels cooling than much to produce some light snow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA potent, negatively-tilted shortwave quickly approaches Sunday \nnight and sends a deepening surface low across New England into \nMonday. Strong lift will allow precipitation, mainly rain, to \nquickly overspread the region overnight and into Monday morning \nbefore tapering off south of the mountains in the afternoon, \nalthough there may still be a few showers after.  In addition to the \nstrong lift, forecast soundings depict a small amount of MUCAPE, so \nrainfall could be heavy at times. As far as amounts, today's \nguidance has trended wetter with higher probabilities for over a \nhalf inch, and there are now also some lower probabilities for more \nthan inch (which is more than what guidance has yesterday). The \nhigher terrain of the Whites and Western Maine mountains could also \npick up some light snow accumulations.\n\nFor the winds with this system: keeping an eye on the late Sunday \nMonday morning timeframe as a 45-55kt S/SSE 925mb LLJ works into the \narea. This could bring some gusty winds to around 35 mph to the \ncoast (particularly the Midcoast) and possibly inland as well, \nespecially as heavier downpours could mix these stronger winds aloft \ndown to the surface. \n\nThe more confident period of stronger winds will be from \nMonday afternoon and Monday evening once the rain tapers off and \nmixing increases, while the pressure gradient also continues to \ntighten between the deepening low pulling east and the building high \nto the west. Probabilities for 40+mph have trended upward in the \nensemble guidance as well as a better signal from the Extreme \nForecast Index (EFI). Based on all of this information, confidence \nis trending higher for a period of west to northwest wind gusts of \n40 to 50 mph and a limited power outage threat Monday afternoon and \nevening. Breezy conditions could last into Monday night, but it's \nuncertain how mixed it will remain.\n\nA colder and drier airmass follows for Tuesday and Wednesday \nwith slightly below normal. Tuesday is not expected to \nbe as breezy as Monday, and then winds will ease later in the \nafternoon as high pressure further builds toward New England. This \nwill also bring any lingering upslope precip in the mountains to an \nend. \n\nEnsembles favor a recovery in high temperatures to near or maybe \neven slightly above normal next Thursday and Friday. There's a small \nchance of rain (20-30%) associated with frontal boundary around \nThursday, and then hints of another system on Friday.",
